{"name":"Sylph","hex":"#adaab1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sylph","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An invisible being of the air.","The elemental being of air, usually female."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sylph"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sylph","blocks":[{"text":"A sylph is an air spirit stemming from the 16th-century works of Paracelsus, who describes sylphs as (invisible) beings of the air, his elementals of air. A significant number of subsequent literary and occult works have been inspired by Paracelsus's concept: Robert Alfred Vaughan noted that \"the wild but poetical fantasies\" of Paracelsus had probably exercised a larger influence over his age and the subsequent one than is generally supposed, particularly on the Rosicrucians, but that through the 18th century they had become reduced to \"machinery for the playwright\" and \"opera figurantes with wings of gauze and spangles\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Praetorius%281666%29-p0000-11Luftleute%26Windmenschen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sylph","wikidata":"Q853329"},{"title":"Sylph (pilot boat)","blocks":[{"text":"The Sylph was a 19th-century pilot boat first built in 1834, by Whitmore & Holbrook for John Perkins Cushing as a Boston yacht and pilot-boat for merchant and ship owner Robert Bennet Forbes. She won the first recorded American yacht race in 1835. She was a pilot boat in the Boston Harbor in 1836 and 1837 and sold to the New York and Sandy Hook Pilots in October 1837. She was lost in winter of 1857 with all hands during a blizzard off Barnegat, New Jersey. The second Sylph was built in 1865 from a half-model by Dennison J. Lawlor. The third Sylph was built in 1878 at Nathaniel Porter Keen Shipyard on the Fore River in North Weymouth, Massachusetts for Boston Pilots. She was sold out of service in 1901, after 23 years of Boston pilot service."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Stebbins-Pilot-boat-Sylph-1878.jpg/330px-Stebbins-Pilot-boat-Sylph-1878.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sylph_(pilot_boat)","wikidata":"Q104862910"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Paracelsus.jpg/500px-Paracelsus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Paracelsus.jpg/1280px-Paracelsus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Paracelsus.jpg/500px-Paracelsus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Swiss alchemist and philosopher Theophrastus von Hohenheim (\"Paracelsus\") first created the concept of the sylph.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParacelsus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg/500px-La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg/1280px-La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg/500px-La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"La Sylphide Bourbon, A.M. Bininger & Co. Bourbon advertising label in the shape of a glass showing a man pursuing three sylphs","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALa_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Sylphide_-Marie_Taglioni_-1832_-2.jpg/500px-Sylphide_-Marie_Taglioni_-1832_-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Sylphide_-Marie_Taglioni_-1832_-2.jpg/500px-Sylphide_-Marie_Taglioni_-1832_-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Marie Taglioni in La Sylphide","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASylphide_-Marie_Taglioni_-1832_-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Sylph_pilot_boat.jpg/500px-Sylph_pilot_boat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Sylph_pilot_boat.jpg/500px-Sylph_pilot_boat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sylph pilot boat with all her sails.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASylph_pilot_boat.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Joseph_W._Colby.jpg/330px-Joseph_W._Colby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Joseph_W._Colby.jpg/330px-Joseph_W._Colby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pilot Joseph W. Colby","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJoseph_W._Colby.jpg"}],"sounds":{"sylph":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6e/En-us-sylph.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/6e/En-us-sylph.ogg/En-us-sylph.ogg.mp3"}]}}}